## Method and reasoning
Start position i with the best border length of s[0..i−1]. If the next character disagrees, fall back to the next longest border via pi[j−1]. Every smaller viable border is a border of the current candidate, so this chain skips only impossible lengths. On a match extend by one. Increases are at most one per position, and each fallback strictly decreases the length, so total work is O(n). The output array takes O(n) space.

## Walk through the sample
At the c of ababcabab the previous candidate has length 2 (ab). It would extend only with a. Falling back to zero still cannot match c, so this entry is zero. The remaining abab rebuilds lengths 1,2,3,4.

## Complexity
Each successful extension increases the border length by one. Every fallback decreases it, so all fallback steps together cost O(n). The prefix table uses O(n) space.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    string s;
    cin >> s;
    int n = s.size();
    vector<int> pi(n);
    for (int i = 1; i < n; ++i) {
        int j = pi[i - 1];
        while (j > 0 && s[i] != s[j])
            j = pi[j - 1];
        if (s[i] == s[j])
            ++j;
        pi[i] = j;
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i)
        cout << pi[i] << (i + 1 == n ? '\n' : ' ');
}
~~~
